WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.721
Hey there! Thanks for coming out today&nbsp;
and joining us to learn a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.721 --> 00:00:08.844
about how you would deploy SAP into the cloud.

00:00:08.844 --> 00:00:12.545
And I know that this is a terrifying incredibly complicated solution.

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:18.351
It's the kind that&nbsp;sends chills and shutters down the back of any CIO who's about to go down this road,

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:20.233
but&nbsp;it doesn't have to be.

00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:24.097
It's a fairly basic process from a conceptual standpoint.

00:00:24.097 --> 00:00:26.397
It's the execution that's the hard part.

00:00:26.397 --> 00:00:30.776
And so, how we're going to answer this is&nbsp;
we're going to go through the logical steps

00:00:30.776 --> 00:00:35.234
of how you actually go through the process of&nbsp;
deploying SAP onto the cloud,

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:38.247
to try and demystify it, to make it a little more straightforward.

00:00:38.247 --> 00:00:41.213
So that you, as you're doing all of your planning,

00:00:41.213 --> 00:00:45.476
can incorporate these steps into figuring out your&nbsp;timelines, figuring out your budgets,

00:00:45.476 --> 00:00:51.215
but most importantly, hopefully you can take away some&nbsp;advice and some lessons learned from this video

00:00:51.215 --> 00:00:55.740
so that you don't have to experience the pain that&nbsp;some of our other clients have had over time.

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:59.294
And so, when we talk about deploying SAP on the&nbsp;cloud,

00:00:59.294 --> 00:01:02.022
probably the first and most important part,

00:01:02.022 --> 00:01:07.451
and arguably the longest part of your process, needs to be number 1

00:01:07.451 --> 00:01:13.710
- and that number 1 is you must evaluate your existing landscape.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:18.188
And when you evaluate that that landscape, it's not just a matter of sizing,

00:01:18.188 --> 00:01:21.477
you know, how&nbsp;big is it today, how big is it going to get in the future -

00:01:21.477 --> 00:01:24.763
if all you talk is tech, you're missing&nbsp;the boat.

00:01:24.763 --> 00:01:28.673
The evaluation piece has to be about your business.

00:01:28.673 --> 00:01:31.260
Moving to the cloud needs to have&nbsp;
a reason.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.000
You don't do it just because it's fun,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.985
or just because you've got extra money that you&nbsp;need to spend.

00:01:35.985 --> 00:01:39.156
You need to define: what are the success criteria?

00:01:39.156 --> 00:01:41.335
What problems am I solving,

00:01:41.335 --> 00:01:44.773
and is moving to the cloud going to answer those problems?

00:01:44.773 --> 00:01:49.314
If you haven't answered those&nbsp;questions yet, don't start on this journey.

00:01:49.314 --> 00:01:51.373
Work on the business challenge first,

00:01:51.373 --> 00:01:54.400
because if&nbsp;the only thing you do is move to cloud

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.143
without addressing any of the other challenges you&nbsp;already have,

00:01:57.143 --> 00:02:00.212
your cloud migration is never going to be successful.

00:02:00.227 --> 00:02:03.612
It's only going to&nbsp;add additional difficulty to your life.

00:02:03.794 --> 00:02:07.906
So, once you've done that evaluation, once you&nbsp;have gotten out,

00:02:07.906 --> 00:02:13.049
and you've said, "These are our goals, this is what we're going to fix, this is&nbsp;our success criteria,

00:02:13.049 --> 00:02:15.138
and this is how we're going&nbsp;about it",

00:02:15.138 --> 00:02:20.059
then it's time to step in and say, "OK,&nbsp;what do we do first?"

00:02:20.059 --> 00:02:23.442
Well, so working with your system integrator, the first thing you're going to&nbsp;do,

00:02:23.442 --> 00:02:26.115
is you're going to bring up some Dev systems, right? 

00:02:26.115 --> 00:02:28.720
So that those Dev systems, these are&nbsp;little.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.603
These are not big systems, they're small systems,

00:02:31.603 --> 00:02:35.764
and the purpose of the Dev systems&nbsp;
is to do some little baby proofs of concept, right?

00:02:35.764 --> 00:02:39.394
You're going to do a little POC work,&nbsp;
you're going to do a little bit of testing on them.

00:02:39.394 --> 00:02:44.125
You're going to make sure that the&nbsp; infrastructure that you think you're going to use

00:02:44.125 --> 00:02:47.742
is going to be successful in the way that you&nbsp; intend to use it.

00:02:47.742 --> 00:02:49.348
That's the purpose of Dev.

00:02:49.348 --> 00:02:53.714
You're also going to start trying to figure out&nbsp;
how to move all my customizations,

00:02:53.714 --> 00:02:55.928
how to move all of my data around,

00:02:55.928 --> 00:03:00.556
because coming from on-premises&nbsp;
into the cloud is not a trivial challenge.

00:03:00.556 --> 00:03:03.790
You need to start doing some of your data mapping with&nbsp;these Dev systems, right?

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:06.788
How do I get data in, how do I get data out?

00:03:06.788 --> 00:03:13.078
And so, once you've got&nbsp;your Dev, then it's time to start bringing up the big systems, right?

00:03:13.078 --> 00:03:15.212
And I'm going to call this&nbsp;"Stage",

00:03:15.212 --> 00:03:19.068
and the reason that I'm going to call it Stage is because eventually

00:03:19.068 --> 00:03:22.720
the second set that&nbsp;you bring up this is going to be your staging environment.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.058
So, a normal flow would go from Dev&nbsp;
into Stage.

00:03:26.058 --> 00:03:29.957
So, your normal SAP progression is going to go from Dev into Stage.

00:03:29.957 --> 00:03:35.548
And so, as&nbsp;you move forward, once you've got your staging environment up, you've got it stable,

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:39.217
this is when&nbsp;you start incorporating all that customization, right?

00:03:39.217 --> 00:03:41.339
And at this point you might not have one&nbsp;
Dev environment.

00:03:41.339 --> 00:03:42.533
You might have 2,

00:03:42.533 --> 00:03:45.200
you might have 3, you might have 4,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.858
but really, it's getting all&nbsp;of your teams involved so that they're all working toward the same goal.

00:03:50.858 --> 00:03:53.770
So, you've got this Stage&nbsp;environment, you're feeding Dev in,

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:58.106
and you say, "OK, well we're ready to really get into some&nbsp;real testing, right?"

00:03:58.106 --> 00:04:01.137
Well, in that case, we're now going to order

00:04:01.137 --> 00:04:05.396
what will eventually&nbsp;be, but isn't today, the DR (Distaster Recovery) environment .

00:04:05.396 --> 00:04:09.995
Because what's the purpose of having a DR if you&nbsp;don't ever actually test it?

00:04:09.995 --> 00:04:15.147
And so, not only are you going to start moving all of this into&nbsp;your DR environment,

00:04:15.147 --> 00:04:18.359
but also let's go ahead and do a sync, right?

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:22.112
Let's get all that on-premises&nbsp;data that we've got,

00:04:22.112 --> 00:04:24.348
and let's get it copied into our DR environment.

00:04:24.348 --> 00:04:29.661
Because, as a DR site, it has&nbsp;to work as production in the event of a disaster,

00:04:29.746 --> 00:04:33.095
so it has to be the same specs as your production&nbsp;system.

00:04:33.095 --> 00:04:37.660
So, let's get a full data load into it, and let's start running some actual performance&nbsp;
testing.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.249
Let's start running some actual load on it.

00:04:40.249 --> 00:04:42.717
Let's start running some actual queries on it

00:04:42.717 --> 00:04:46.571
to be sure that it does, in fact, solve the problems that you need.

00:04:46.571 --> 00:04:48.960
Let's also be sure that all of&nbsp;
our data pathing is right.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.583
Let's be sure that it has access to what it needs,

00:04:51.583 --> 00:04:55.130
and that the data&nbsp;that's flowing to it is flowing to it correctly.

00:04:55.285 --> 00:04:57.864
Next, once we're happy here,

00:04:57.864 --> 00:05:01.361
now we're&nbsp;going to move up and we're going to get&nbsp;&nbsp;our Pre-Prod systems up.

00:05:01.361 --> 00:05:06.129
So, your Pre-Prod is&nbsp;going to be slightly downsized from production,

00:05:06.129 --> 00:05:09.200
but the goal is confirmation testing, right?

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.688
Your&nbsp;eventual flow will be Dev, Stage, Pre-Prod, Prod.

00:05:12.688 --> 00:05:15.446
But for now, we're going to stop in this DR&nbsp;environment,

00:05:15.446 --> 00:05:18.113
we're going to do some testing, and we're going to get Pre-Prod stable, right?

00:05:18.113 --> 00:05:22.335
We're&nbsp;going to get our DevOps path from Dev, to Stage, to Pre-Prod.

00:05:22.335 --> 00:05:23.435
We're going to get that solid.

00:05:23.435 --> 00:05:25.568
We're going to be sure that our release process is right.

00:05:25.568 --> 00:05:27.941
We're going to be sure that our change&nbsp;
control is right,

00:05:27.941 --> 00:05:31.640
because then it's time to go up

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:38.816
and let's get your production system which is the&nbsp;most important part, right?

00:05:38.816 --> 00:05:41.136
So, you're going to go up to your Prod system,

00:05:41.136 --> 00:05:44.631
and since you've got&nbsp;Prod, guess what? Time to bring data in again!

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:49.920
So, we're going to get another fresh copy of&nbsp;
data from our existing on-premises solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.840
and we're going to get that loaded into&nbsp;
production, and then we're going to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.588
all of our end-to-end testing.

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:59.492
All of our user&nbsp;acceptance testing.
All of our criteria.

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:03.213
So that we can get to the holy grail of Go Live,

00:06:03.213 --> 00:06:06.188
and the whole point of getting to Go Live,

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:09.214
is this is when you do your cut over, right?

00:06:09.214 --> 00:06:13.214
You've already done your sync to Prod, so you've just got to get a little delta on your&nbsp;data.

00:06:13.214 --> 00:06:17.590
Once you get that delta load, you're going to have to take some sort of a downtime outage

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.441
to cut over into your new production system.

00:06:20.593 --> 00:06:25.296
Once you have done that, your environment is&nbsp;
up, it s stable, it's ready to go.

00:06:25.296 --> 00:06:29.754
At that point you're going to go back into a normal Dev&nbsp;cycle, right,

00:06:29.754 --> 00:06:32.842
into that continuous integration, continuous release cycle

00:06:32.842 --> 00:06:35.335
where you're feeding&nbsp;Dev changes into Stage,

00:06:35.335 --> 00:06:37.483
and you don't have to stop at DR anymore,

00:06:37.483 --> 00:06:41.820
but your changes in Stage&nbsp;are going to go to Pre-Prod, Pre-Prod into Prod,

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:43.377
and then start back again.

00:06:43.377 --> 00:06:46.772
So, all of this&nbsp;work is going to continue as time goes on,

00:06:46.772 --> 00:06:49.197
and you're going to be in that wonderful&nbsp;cycle,

00:06:49.197 --> 00:06:51.411
but that's how you get from on-premises

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:57.510
into the cloud, in a way that is&nbsp;
hopefully solving all of your problems.

00:06:58.035 --> 00:07:01.200
Thank you so much for&nbsp;
stopping by the channel today!

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:05.200
If you have any questions or comments,&nbsp;
please feel free to share them with us below.

